      "Language": "chi",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "The effects of primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI) in patients with acute myocardial infarction (AMI) induced by left main (LM) artery occlusion were analyzed retrospectively in this study."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "A total of 1343 consecutive AMI patients who underwent primary PCI between January 1995 and December 2004 were retrospectively studied."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "LM occlusion or severe stenosis were found in 11 patients [all male, mean age (56.4 +/- 9.2) years (range 43-70 years)], cardiogenic shock was overt in 6 patients. Primary PCI were performed under the assistance of intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) in these patients [8 stent implantation, 3 balloon dilation and 2 necessitating emergency CABG after balloon dilation]. In-hospital mortality was 45.5% (5/11). Three-month follow-up were made in all survivals (6/11). Analysis showed good collateral circulation flow from right coronary artery to left coronary artery was existed in all survival cases before PCI."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Prognosis of AMI patients with LM artery obstruction or severe stenosis was poor. Patients with pre-existed collateral circulation before primary PCI and IABP had a better clinical outcomes."
        }
      ]},
